  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nAs of 1230am, there is an impressive supercell moving eastward into \nPotter/Sully county, which is located along the northwest side of \nthe 850mb warm air advection that stretches from there and to the \nsoutheast towards southeastern SD. There were other cells that \ndeveloped along the WAA to the southeast of this main storm, but \nthey've since dissipated, but do have other storms to the northwest \nof this one which are associated with a shortwave noted on WV \nimagery. With the forcing likely to continue to push slowly to the \neast-northeast through the night and plenty of instability (2k J/kg) \nand shear (40-50kts), not too much to inhibit this storm from \ncontinuing to the east tonight and will likely come down to storm \nscale processes over the coming hours. The rest of the convection in \nnorth central SD will lift to the northeast with time this morning.\n\nBehind the early this morning elevated convection, will continue to \nsee a broad surface low over South Dakota through the day. One of \nthe main questions for the eastern part of the area is where the \nwarm front and associated WAA will settle through the day, as this \ncould lead to some development farther east this afternoon than the \nprimary threat that will be moving through the north central part of \nSD. This area along the warm front will have ample instability (1500-\n2500 J/kg of MUCAPE) and shear (40kts), with continued elongated \nhodographs leading to the potential for an isolated supercell. Of \ncourse, anything occurring right along the warm front could lead to \na brief tornado threat too. Now for the primary potential for \nconvection this afternoon and evening. The upper low near \nWA/ID/MT/Canada border area will move to the east-northeast through \nthe day today and into southern Saskatchewan by this evening. The \nfalling heights and cooling temperatures aloft will be strongest to \nthe northwest of the area and lead to the convection developing \nupstream and along the cold front moving easterly through MT/WY this \nafternoon. As the storms move eastward this evening and into the \nDakotas (and some likely developing out ahead of the front), they'll \nencounter increasing instability (going from 500-1000 J/kg to 1000-\n2000 J/kg) as they move into western ND and northern SD. The \ncombination of the forcing and the increase in instability expect \nboth an increase in coverage (towards linear mode) and a continued \npotential for severe storms. This will likely lead to the severe \nthreat shifting from hail/wind to more wind threat with time. Expect \nthe severe threat to be focused in north central SD and northward, \nwith the primary questions being how far south and to the east it \nwill go. It will either be fighting less forcing (farther south \noption) or increase in nocturnal inversion (farther east). CAMS in \ngeneral are fairly consistent in a limitation in the southward \nextent (generally north of US-212), but vary in the eastward extent \n(some dying things in north central SD and others moving it into \nnorth central SD). So will need to continue to fine tune the \nexpectations through the day today. \n\nThe upper level and surface low will continue to the northeast on \nMonday, allowing high pressure to build into the region. Expect the \ntight pressure gradient over eastern SD to diminish quickly Monday \nmorning and winds will be light over the area under the high for the \nrest of Monday. Slightly cooler temps will be in place for Monday, \nalthough still being around or slightly above normal (low-mid 80s). \nDrier air will also move in, with dewpoints falling into the low-mid \n50s, so will be a pretty nice and comfortable day. That may change \ngoing into Tuesday, as a shortwave rounds and exits the upper trough \noff the west coast and moves through the northern/central Plains. \nThere does appear to be a more northerly shift to this wave and the \nresultant surface features (elongated surface low from somewhere \nbetween southeast SD and southeast NE), leading to an increase in \nthe precipitation chances compared to 24hrs ago (now 40-60% vs ~20% \n24hrs ago). There is the potential for some heavy rain with this \nwave, as NBM 90th percentile 24hr QPF amounts are in the 1-2\" range \n(75th percentile is 0.25-1\"), with the highest amounts over the \nsoutheast half of the CWA. As for severe, ML output does hint at \nsome lower probabilities of severe weather with this wave (ensembles \nhave 1-2k J/kg of CAPE and 30-40kts of shear), so will need to \nmonitor that potential over the coming days. \n\nBehind that wave, the upper ridge will build over the north central \nCONUS, although there still continues to be uncertainty on the ridge \naxis. Latest trends are for it to be around MN and amplifies through \nthe end of the week. That will lead to temperatures warming into the \nmid-upper 80s to low 90s across the area. At this time, not too much \nfor signals for precipitation for the second half of next week and \ninto next weekend,",
